---
import ProsePage from '../../templates/ProsePage.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes.ts';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../config/site.ts';
---

<ProsePage
  locale="en"
  routeKey="about"
  title="About twente.dev"
  description="Twente's practitioner-led technology community: what it is, what it will never be, and how it is governed."
>
  <p>
    For people who build and apply technology in Twente, twente.dev is the independent community
    that makes local knowledge, people and opportunities easier to find — because useful regional
    connections should not depend on already knowing the room.
  </p>
  <p>
    Good work is happening here, often behind organisational walls. Post-pandemic habits reduced the
    accidental encounters, and the communities that exist became harder to discover together.
    twente.dev is the shared front door: a calendar, a directory, an archive, a newsletter, and a
    small number of deliberately cross-disciplinary events —
    <a href={routePath('edition001', 'en')}>twente.dev/001</a> is the first.
  </p>

  <h2>Five choices we hold ourselves to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Independent over corporate.</strong> Businesses fund and host, but never own.</li>
    <li><strong>Practitioner over policy.</strong> Field reports from people who did the work.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Broad network over broad agenda.</strong> Software, hardware, data, security, design, product,
      research and education — with editorial standards, not an everything-stage.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Contribution over consumption.</strong> Bring one thing you learned, one thing you need,
      or one person others should know.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Regional depth over national scale.</strong> Twente first, properly.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we will not do</h2>
  <p>
    No paid speaking slots. No attendee-list access for anyone. No product pitches disguised as
    education. No sponsor approval over editorial decisions. And no pretending existing meetups
    failed — they keep their identity, their list and their stage;
    <a href={routePath('partners', 'en')}>we make them easier to find</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Governance</h2>
  <p>
    twente.dev is being set up as an independent foundation (stichting) with its own board, bank
    account and books. Until the notarial deed is signed, the community is run by its founding team,
    with the same safeguards already in force: partner money buys credits and places, never data,
    stage time or editorial say. After every flagship event we publish a public report — including
    what did not work — with a financial summary.
  </p>
  <p>
    And because it is the first question you should ask: <strong>who pays for this?</strong> Ryan Grippeling
    funds twente.dev out of his own pocket and runs it through his own one-person business (Webgrip).
    His employer, Code14, contributes time and a small marketing budget. That is the entire list. None
    of them buys stage time, attendee data or editorial influence — the same rule applies to everyone,
    and most of all to the people closest to the project.
  </p>

  <h2>Bilingual, and that is not a detail</h2>
  <p>
    A substantial share of the people building technology here arrived via the University of Twente
    and do not (yet) speak Dutch. Community content is English-first, practical information exists
    in both languages, and where an article only exists in one language we say so plainly instead of
    machine-translating it.
  </p>

  <h2>No cookies, no tracking</h2>
  <p>
    This site sets no cookies and loads no third-party scripts, so there is no consent banner
    either. Visitor numbers come from an aggregate that cannot identify individuals. The
    <a href={routePath('privacy', 'en')}>privacy page</a> covers events and the newsletter.
  </p>

  <h2>Everything lives in git</h2>
  <p>
    Every event, company profile and article is a file in a public repository. You can see when
    something was added, by whom, and what changed since. That is the difference between a community
    site and a platform.
  </p>

  <h2>Taking part</h2>
  <p>
    The community grows by contribution: a story, a demo, an introduction, a listed event, a
    volunteer shift. Start at <a href={routePath('contribute', 'en')}>contribute</a>, or email
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>
</ProsePage>
